## 21. ENGINEERING ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

A capability is complete only when its necessary implementation and evidence exist.

| Capability | Acceptance evidence |
| --- | --- |
| Repository preservation | Existing architecture and history retained |
| CI | Real tests executed with reliable failure propagation |
| Governance gate | Actual baseline-versus-candidate drift enforcement |
| Database migrations | Reproducible clean and upgrade-path tests |
| Idempotency | Duplicate and retry integration tests |
| Ledger | State-transition and concurrency tests |
| Recovery | Reproducible stale-run or crash-recovery scenario |
| Reconciliation | Positive and negative consistency tests |
| dbt | Actual model execution and data-test evidence |
| Data quality | Defects cause meaningful test failures |
| Runtime lineage | Execution-to-dataset relationships demonstrated |
| Privacy | Forbidden data rejected and not leaked |
| Observability | Real operational metrics or evidence queries |
| Pandas / NumPy | Genuine tested processing use case |
| Scikit-learn | Reproducible operational experiment with limitations |
| Documentation | All claims traceable to code or executed evidence |

**Acceptance rules:**

- Missing evidence means NOT VERIFIED.
- An unavailable runtime means BLOCKED, not PASSED.
- Partial implementation means INCOMPLETE.
- A mock-only test cannot prove database integration.
- A generated diagram cannot prove system functionality.
- A documented command cannot prove that the command succeeded.
- A green unit-test suite alone does not establish end-to-end correctness.

